  <h2>The Punch List We Run on Every Electrician Profile</h2>
  <p>
    Think of it like a rough-in checklist. There are a handful of things Google looks at, and most
    electrical shops have never finished them. The main category is the big one, we have seen that
    single setting turn the phones on and off. After that: how close your listed address is to the
    work you want, the words in your business name, showing a real address rather than hiding it,
    listing every service you offer, having your name, address and phone match your website, and a
    steady trickle of genuine reviews.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ranking is a competition, not a checklist you tick once. But because so few shops finish the
    basics, doing them properly is usually enough to move you. After that we keep going with
    content, links, and pages for the towns you want more work in.
  </p>

  <h2>Seeing Where You Rank, Street by Street</h2>
  <p>
    Instead of one made-up ranking number, we run a grid across your service area and check your
    position from dozens of points on the map, at the time of day your trade actually gets searched.
    For a lot of electricians that is first thing in the morning. Being in the top three in the
    neighbourhoods that matter brings in calls at a fraction of what ads cost. Rankings move around
    as competitors and reviews change, so when a pocket of the map stays dark, we add a page or push
    reviews in that area.
  </p>

  <h2>Google Reviews for Electricians, Done Honestly</h2>
  <p>
    A one to three man shop wins with a simple habit: ask at the end of every job, with a reminder
    from your job software. Bigger crews need that same habit written down so it does not depend on
    you chasing people. We coach your team on how to ask, encourage photos, and keep it consistent.
    We do not buy reviews and we would tell you not to either. Google looks at how many, how recent,
    and how real they are, and shops that game it eventually get caught.
  </p>
